  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "The expression \\(2+\\sqrt2+\\dfrac1{2+\\sqrt2}+\\dfrac1{\\sqrt2-2}\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(2-\\sqrt2\\)",
    "C": "\\(2+\\sqrt2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\sqrt2\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Combine the two reciprocal terms first. Their denominators form a sum-and-difference pair, so their product simplifies by the difference-of-squares identity:\n\\[\n(2+\\sqrt2)(\\sqrt2-2)\n=(\\sqrt2)^2-2^2=-2.\n\\]\nIn particular, neither denominator is zero. Using their product as a common denominator gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{2+\\sqrt2}+\\frac1{\\sqrt2-2}\n=\\frac{(\\sqrt2-2)+(2+\\sqrt2)}{-2}\n=-\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nHence the entire expression is\n\\[\n2+\\sqrt2-\\sqrt2=2,\n\\]\nwhich is choice A.",
